Chapter: X509Chain
  cites Encode chapter X509
  cites Encode chapter Asn1
  cites Foreword chapter Ed25519
  cites Foreword chapter Rsa
  cites Foreword chapter EcdsaP256
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The issuer link is compared as RAW DER BYTES, never as decoded text. Two
 names that print the same can be different encodings, and a decoder that
 normalises them is a decoder that can be talked into believing one
 authority is another.

 An issuer must be a CA. A leaf certificate that is allowed to sign
 another certificate is an authority, and every certificate it signs is
 trusted. This is the single most valuable bit in the whole structure, and
 it is one bit.

 An issuer's key must be able to VERIFY. An X25519 key agrees keys; it
 signs nothing. X509 makes that a type, and this chapter honours it.

 We say:

Section: Verdict

  X509Verdict =
    | ChainOk
    | ChainEmpty
    | ChainNotYetValid
    | ChainExpired
    | ChainBadTime
    | ChainBadSignature
    | ChainBrokenIssuer
    | ChainIssuerNotCa
    | ChainIssuerCannotSign
    | ChainNoAnchor
    | ChainTooLong
    | ChainWrongIdentity

 A trust anchor is a name and a key. It is not a certificate: an anchor is
 trusted because the operator put it there, not because something signed
 it, and modelling it as a certificate invites somebody to go looking for
 its issuer.

  X509Anchor = record {
    xa-subject : List Integer,
    xa-key : X509Key
  }

 A chain longer than this is refused rather than walked. The bound is not
 a performance concern -- it is that an unbounded walk over attacker-
 supplied certificates is an attacker-supplied loop.

  x509-max-chain : Integer = 8

Section: Time

 A certificate time is either a UTCTime (YYMMDDHHMMSSZ, 13 bytes) or a
 GeneralizedTime (YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ, 15 bytes). Both normalise to fourteen
 ASCII digits, which compare correctly as bytes because every field is
 fixed width and zero padded.

 RFC 5280 section 4.1.2.5.1: in a UTCTime, a two-digit year of 50 or more
 means 19xx and anything less means 20xx. Getting this backwards makes a
 certificate from 1996 look like one that expires in 2096.

  x509-time-norm : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-time-norm (t) =
    if list-length t == 13 then x509-utc-time t
    else if list-length t == 15 then x509-gen-time t
    else None

  x509-ascii-z : Integer = 90
  x509-ascii-0 : Integer = 48
  x509-ascii-9 : Integer = 57

  x509-utc-time : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-utc-time (t) =
    if list-at t 12 /= x509-ascii-z then None
    else if x509-all-digits t 0 12 == False then None
    else Just (x509-century (list-at t 0) & x509-take t 0 12)

 The century bytes, as ASCII: "19" or "20".

  x509-century : Integer -> List Integer
  x509-century (y0) =
    if y0 >= x509-ascii-0 + 5 then [49, 57] else [50, 48]

  x509-gen-time : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-gen-time (t) =
    if list-at t 14 /= x509-ascii-z then None
    else if x509-all-digits t 0 14 == False then None
    else Just (x509-take t 0 14)

  x509-all-digits : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  x509-all-digits (t) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then True
    else if list-at t i < x509-ascii-0 then False
    else if list-at t i > x509-ascii-9 then False
    else x509-all-digits t (i + 1) n

  x509-take : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  x509-take (t) (off) (n) = x509-take-loop t off n 0 []

  x509-take-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  x509-take-loop (t) (off) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if off + i >= list-length t then acc
    else x509-take-loop t off n (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at t (off + i)))

  x509-time-le : List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  x509-time-le (a) (b) = x509-le-loop a b 0

  x509-le-loop : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  x509-le-loop (a) (b) (i) =
    if i >= list-length a then True
    else if i >= list-length b then False
    else if list-at a i < list-at b i then True
    else if list-at a i > list-at b i then False
    else x509-le-loop a b (i + 1)

  x509-valid-at : X509Cert, List Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-valid-at (c) (now) =
    when x509-time-norm (c.xc-not-before)
      is None -> ChainBadTime
      is Just (nb) -> x509-valid-after c now nb

  x509-valid-after : X509Cert, List Integer, List Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-valid-after (c) (now) (nb) =
    when x509-time-norm (c.xc-not-after)
      is None -> ChainBadTime
      is Just (na) -> x509-window nb na now

  x509-window : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-window (nb) (na) (now) =
    if x509-time-le nb now == False then ChainNotYetValid
    else if x509-time-le now na == False then ChainExpired
    else ChainOk

Section: Signature

 A certificate is signed over its tbsCertificate -- the bytes the parser
 kept, not a re-encoding of them. The key must be one that can sign at all.

 The certificate says what it was signed with and the issuer's key says
 what it can verify with, and BOTH have to agree before anything is
 checked. Taking the algorithm from only one of them is how a signature
 gets verified under a scheme its author never chose.

  x509-signed-by : X509Cert, X509Key -> Boolean
  x509-signed-by (c) (k) =
    if x509-can-verify k == False then False
    else x509-verify-with (c.xc-sig-alg) c k

 SHA-1 answers False and always will. The certificate parses, because a
 root signed with it in 2006 is still a root we may choose to hold; but a
 chain link resting on a SHA-1 signature is not verified here. Chosen-
 prefix collisions have been practical since 2017, and an algorithm that
 cannot distinguish two documents cannot attest to either.

  x509-verify-with : X509SigAlg, X509Cert, X509Key -> Boolean
  x509-verify-with (salg) (c) (k) =
    when salg
      is SigUnknown -> False
      is SigRsaSha1 -> False
      is SigEcdsaSha384 -> x509-verify-ecdsa384 c k
      is SigEd25519 -> x509-verify-ed c k
      is SigRsaSha256 -> x509-verify-rsa c k
      is SigRsaSha384 -> x509-verify-rsa384 c k
      is SigEcdsaSha256 -> x509-verify-ecdsa c k

  x509-verify-ed : X509Cert, X509Key -> Boolean
  x509-verify-ed (c) (k) =
    when k.xk-alg
      is KeyEd25519 -> ed25519-verify (k.xk-bytes) (c.xc-tbs) (c.xc-sig)
      is otherwise -> False

  x509-verify-rsa : X509Cert, X509Key -> Boolean
  x509-verify-rsa (c) (k) =
    when k.xk-alg
      is KeyRsa -> rsa-verify-pkcs1-sha256 (k.xk-mod) (k.xk-exp) (c.xc-tbs) (c.xc-sig)
      is otherwise -> False

  x509-verify-rsa384 : X509Cert, X509Key -> Boolean
  x509-verify-rsa384 (c) (k) =
    when k.xk-alg
      is KeyRsa -> rsa-verify-pkcs1-sha384 (k.xk-mod) (k.xk-exp) (c.xc-tbs) (c.xc-sig)
      is otherwise -> False

  x509-verify-ecdsa : X509Cert, X509Key -> Boolean
  x509-verify-ecdsa (c) (k) =
    when k.xk-alg
      is KeyEcdsaP256 -> x509-ecdsa-go (k.xk-bytes) (c.xc-tbs) (x509-ecdsa-rs (c.xc-sig) 32)
      is KeyEcdsaP384 -> x509-p384-sha256-go (k.xk-bytes) (c.xc-tbs) (x509-ecdsa-rs (c.xc-sig) 48)
      is otherwise -> False

  x509-ecdsa-go : List Integer, List Integer, Maybe (List Integer) -> Boolean
  x509-ecdsa-go (pub) (tbs) (m) =
    when m
      is None -> False
      is Just (rs) -> ecdsa-p256-verify pub tbs rs

 The SHA-384 arm is the same walk against the other digest. It is written
 out rather than folded into the one above with a flag, because the
 digest a certificate declares and the digest we compute must be the same
 one, and a boolean parameter is exactly how those come apart.

  x509-verify-ecdsa384 : X509Cert, X509Key -> Boolean
  x509-verify-ecdsa384 (c) (k) =
    when k.xk-alg
      is KeyEcdsaP256 -> x509-ecdsa384-go (k.xk-bytes) (c.xc-tbs) (x509-ecdsa-rs (c.xc-sig) 32)
      is KeyEcdsaP384 -> x509-p384-sha384-go (k.xk-bytes) (c.xc-tbs) (x509-ecdsa-rs (c.xc-sig) 48)
      is otherwise -> False

  x509-ecdsa384-go : List Integer, List Integer, Maybe (List Integer) -> Boolean
  x509-ecdsa384-go (pub) (tbs) (m) =
    when m
      is None -> False
      is Just (rs) -> ecdsa-p256-verify-sha384 pub tbs rs

  x509-p384-sha256-go : List Integer, List Integer, Maybe (List Integer) -> Boolean
  x509-p384-sha256-go (pub) (tbs) (m) =
    when m
      is None -> False
      is Just (rs) -> ecdsa-p384-verify pub tbs rs

  x509-p384-sha384-go : List Integer, List Integer, Maybe (List Integer) -> Boolean
  x509-p384-sha384-go (pub) (tbs) (m) =
    when m
      is None -> False
      is Just (rs) -> ecdsa-p384-verify-sha384 pub tbs rs

Section: Issuers

  x509-issuer-ok : X509Cert -> X509Verdict
  x509-issuer-ok (issuer) =
    if issuer.xc-is-ca == False then ChainIssuerNotCa
    else if x509-can-verify (issuer.xc-key) == False then ChainIssuerCannotSign
    else ChainOk

Section: Names

  x509-name-eq : List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  x509-name-eq (a) (b) =
    if list-length a /= list-length b then False
    else x509-name-loop a b 0 (list-length a)

  x509-name-loop : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  x509-name-loop (a) (b) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then True
    else if list-at a i /= list-at b i then False
    else x509-name-loop a b (i + 1) n

Section: Peer Identity

 A chain that walks to a trusted anchor says the certificate is genuine.
 It does not say the certificate is THIS PEER'S. Until 2026-07-26 nothing
 in the tree asked the second question and there was nowhere to ask it
 from -- neither the client endpoint nor this function took the name that
 had been dialled -- so any certificate from any trusted authority
 authenticated for every host. That is the oldest mistake in transport
 security (RFC 8446 s4.4.2.4, RFC 6125 s6) and it is why `expected` is a
 parameter of x509-chain-verify rather than an option beside it: a caller
 that cannot say who it expected cannot be told whether it got them.

 Matching is on subjectAltName and nothing else. An IPv4 literal is
 matched against iPAddress entries and a name against dNSName entries,
 because a certificate for the name `10.0.2.2` is not a certificate for
 the address 10.0.2.2 and treating them alike is how an attacker picks
 which one to be. A wildcard matches exactly one label and only in the
 leftmost position, so `*.example.com` covers `a.example.com` and covers
 neither `example.com` nor `a.b.example.com`.

  x509-identity-matches : X509Cert, List Integer -> Boolean
  x509-identity-matches (leaf) (expected) =
    if list-length expected == 0 then False
    else when x509-ipv4-of expected
      is Just (quad) -> x509-any-ip-eq (leaf.xc-ip-addrs) quad 0
      is None -> x509-any-dns-match (leaf.xc-dns-names) expected 0

  x509-any-ip-eq : List (List Integer), List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  x509-any-ip-eq (ips) (quad) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ips then False
    else if x509-id-bytes-eq (list-at ips i) quad then True
    else x509-any-ip-eq ips quad (i + 1)

  x509-id-bytes-eq : List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  x509-id-bytes-eq (a) (b) =
    if list-length a /= list-length b then False
    else x509-id-bytes-loop a b 0

  x509-id-bytes-loop : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  x509-id-bytes-loop (a) (b) (i) =
    if i >= list-length a then True
    else if list-at a i /= list-at b i then False
    else x509-id-bytes-loop a b (i + 1)

  x509-any-dns-match : List (List Integer), List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  x509-any-dns-match (names) (expected) (i) =
    if i >= list-length names then False
    else if x509-dns-match (list-at names i) expected then True
    else x509-any-dns-match names expected (i + 1)

  x509-dns-match : List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  x509-dns-match (cert-name) (expected) =
    if x509-is-wildcard cert-name then x509-wildcard-match cert-name expected
    else x509-ci-eq cert-name expected

  x509-is-wildcard : List Integer -> Boolean
  x509-is-wildcard (n) =
    if list-length n < 3 then False
    else if list-at n 0 /= 42 then False
    else list-at n 1 == 46

  x509-wildcard-match : List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  x509-wildcard-match (cert-name) (expected) =
    let d = x509-first-dot expected 0
    in if d < 1 then False
       else x509-ci-eq (x509-id-drop cert-name 1) (x509-id-drop expected d)

  x509-first-dot : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  x509-first-dot (bs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length bs then -1
    else if list-at bs i == 46 then i
    else x509-first-dot bs (i + 1)

  x509-id-drop : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  x509-id-drop (bs) (n) = x509-id-drop-loop bs n []

  x509-id-drop-loop : List Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  x509-id-drop-loop (bs) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length bs then acc
    else x509-id-drop-loop bs (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at bs i))

  x509-ci-eq : List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  x509-ci-eq (a) (b) =
    if list-length a /= list-length b then False
    else x509-ci-loop a b 0

  x509-ci-loop : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  x509-ci-loop (a) (b) (i) =
    if i >= list-length a then True
    else if x509-lower (list-at a i) /= x509-lower (list-at b i) then False
    else x509-ci-loop a b (i + 1)

  x509-lower : Integer -> Integer
  x509-lower (c) = if c < 65 then c else if c > 90 then c else c + 32

 A dotted quad, or nothing. Four decimal numbers, each at most three
 digits and at most 255, separated by exactly three dots, with no other
 byte permitted -- so `10.0.2.2` is an address and `10.0.2.2.example.com`
 is a name.

  x509-ipv4-of : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-ipv4-of (bs) = x509-ip-scan bs 0 0 0 []

  x509-ip-scan : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-ip-scan (bs) (i) (val) (digits) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length bs then x509-ip-end val digits acc
    else if list-at bs i == 46 then x509-ip-dot bs i val digits acc
    else if list-at bs i < 48 then None
    else if list-at bs i > 57 then None
    else if digits >= 3 then None
    else x509-ip-scan bs (i + 1) (val * 10 + (list-at bs i - 48)) (digits + 1) acc

  x509-ip-dot : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-ip-dot (bs) (i) (val) (digits) (acc) =
    if digits == 0 then None
    else if val > 255 then None
    else if list-length acc >= 3 then None
    else x509-ip-scan bs (i + 1) 0 0 (list-push acc val)

  x509-ip-end : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  x509-ip-end (val) (digits) (acc) =
    if digits == 0 then None
    else if val > 255 then None
    else if list-length acc /= 3 then None
    else Just (list-push acc val)

Section: The Walk

 The chain is leaf first. Each certificate is checked against the next one
 up; the last one is checked against a trust anchor. A chain that reaches
 no anchor is refused -- there is no "well, it looked fine" ending.

  x509-chain-verify : List X509Cert, List X509Anchor, List Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-chain-verify (chain) (anchors) (now) =
    if list-length chain < 1 then ChainEmpty
    else if list-length chain > x509-max-chain then ChainTooLong
    else x509-walk chain anchors now 0

 Path validation and identity are two procedures, not one: RFC 5280 says
 whether a chain is genuine, RFC 6125 says whether it is the peer's. They
 are kept apart here because they answer different questions and because a
 walk has no opinion about hostnames.

 **This is the one an endpoint calls.** x509-chain-verify above proves a
 chain genuine and says nothing about whose it is; a transport that stops
 there authenticates a stranger holding a real certificate. The name is a
 parameter with no permissive value -- an empty expected name matches
 nothing, because a peer you cannot name is a peer you cannot verify.

  x509-verify-peer : List X509Cert, List X509Anchor, List Integer, List Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-verify-peer (chain) (anchors) (now) (expected) =
    if list-length chain < 1 then ChainEmpty
    else if x509-identity-matches (list-at chain 0) expected == False then ChainWrongIdentity
    else x509-chain-verify chain anchors now

  x509-walk : List X509Cert, List X509Anchor, List Integer, Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-walk (chain) (anchors) (now) (i) =
    when x509-valid-at (list-at chain i) now
      is ChainOk -> x509-walk-link chain anchors now i
      is otherwise -> x509-valid-at (list-at chain i) now

  x509-walk-link : List X509Cert, List X509Anchor, List Integer, Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-walk-link (chain) (anchors) (now) (i) =
    if i == list-length chain - 1 then x509-to-anchor (list-at chain i) anchors 0
    else x509-to-issuer chain anchors now i

  x509-to-issuer : List X509Cert, List X509Anchor, List Integer, Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-to-issuer (chain) (anchors) (now) (i) =
    when x509-issuer-ok (list-at chain (i + 1))
      is ChainOk -> x509-issuer-link chain anchors now i
      is otherwise -> x509-issuer-ok (list-at chain (i + 1))

  x509-issuer-link : List X509Cert, List X509Anchor, List Integer, Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-issuer-link (chain) (anchors) (now) (i) =
    if x509-name-eq ((list-at chain i).xc-issuer) ((list-at chain (i + 1)).xc-subject) == False
    then ChainBrokenIssuer
    else if x509-signed-by (list-at chain i) ((list-at chain (i + 1)).xc-key) == False
    then ChainBadSignature
    else x509-walk chain anchors now (i + 1)

 An anchor matches on its name, and then it must actually have signed the
 thing. A name match alone proves nothing at all -- anyone can put any name
 in an issuer field.

  x509-to-anchor : X509Cert, List X509Anchor, Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-to-anchor (c) (anchors) (i) =
    if i >= list-length anchors then ChainNoAnchor
    else if x509-name-eq (c.xc-issuer) ((list-at anchors i).xa-subject) == False
    then x509-to-anchor c anchors (i + 1)
    else x509-anchor-sig c anchors i

 The name matched. If the signature does not, we do NOT go looking for
 another anchor with the same name -- that is an oracle, and it lets an
 attacker who can register a colliding name grind against every key we
 hold. One anchor, one answer.

  x509-anchor-sig : X509Cert, List X509Anchor, Integer -> X509Verdict
  x509-anchor-sig (c) (anchors) (i) =
    if x509-signed-by c ((list-at anchors i).xa-key) then ChainOk
    else ChainBadSignature
